WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:11.310
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to this deep dive into Reza Shah Kazemi's spiritual ethos, exploring the Imam, peace be upon him, and his teaching that the peak of remembering God turns out to be a forgetting of the self.

2
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:15.790
<v Speaker 1>The invocation comes forth firstly from the invoked and secondly from the invoker.

3
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:24.270
<v Speaker 1>Today we are unpacking why self-forgetting is the absolute summit of remembrance, and we will finally confront what the name a person invokes truly is.

4
00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.790
<v Speaker 2>It is quite the threshold to cross.

5
00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:34.990
<v Speaker 2>Especially because up to this point in our exploration of the source material, the listener has been guided to build what is called a perfect remembrance.

6
00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.110
<v Speaker 1>I mean, the sheer effort required for that perfect remembrance is staggering on its own.

7
00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:39.710
<v Speaker 2>It really is.

8
00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:43.390
<v Speaker 2>It is this painstaking, lifelong alignment of the heart and the tongue.

9
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.830
<v Speaker 1>So essentially what is concealed perfectly matches what is revealed.

10
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.550
<v Speaker 1>You have a person who has done the grueling work of the greater striving against the ego.

11
00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:57.630
<v Speaker 1>They have spent years, maybe decades, taking the technical act of invocation and using it

12
00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.230
<v Speaker 1>polish the rust from their heart.

13
00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:05.230
<v Speaker 2>Let's linger on that rust for a second, because the text frames that impurity very specifically.

14
00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:11.230
<v Speaker 2>It is any trace of a wish for something other than God within your worship.

15
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.870
<v Speaker 1>Which is an incredibly harsh standard if you think about it.

16
00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:21.630
<v Speaker 1>means if you are sitting there doing your prayers and there is even a microscopic part of you hoping someone notices how pious you are.

17
00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.150
<v Speaker 2>Or even if you were just secretly congratulating yourself for doing the prayer at all.

18
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.350
<v Speaker 1>That is rust.

19
00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:27.870
<v Speaker 1>That is the impurity.

20
00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:33.310
<v Speaker 1>And the greater striving is the agonizing work of scraping that away until the heart is polished.

21
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.190
<v Speaker 1>And when it is polished, the virtues begin to shine.

22
00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.510
<v Speaker 1>It looks like the ultimate human perfection.

23
00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:39.710
<v Speaker 2>It looks like the finish line.

24
00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:40.510
<v Speaker 1>It does.

25
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.270
<v Speaker 1>But here is the crisis the text introduces.

26
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:51.870
<v Speaker 1>The Imam says that even this perfection, this beautiful alignment where your heart and tongue are one, is not the true reality of remembrance.

27
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:52.990
<v Speaker 2>There is something past it.

28
00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.590
<v Speaker 1>There is a final threshold.

29
00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.550
<v Speaker 1>And crossing it demands A profound

30
00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:02.310
<v Speaker 1>and frankly, shocking costs, the complete erasure of the very self that did all that work to reach it.

31
00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.710
<v Speaker 2>To the self that polished the heart.

32
00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:04.990
<v Speaker 1>Must be abandoned entirely.

33
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:05.790
<v Speaker 2>Okay, wait.

34
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:15.630
<v Speaker 1>To you, the listener who might be trying to polish your own heart, you need to understand that passing from human perfection to divine reality is not just a gradual polishing.

35
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.350
<v Speaker 1>You do not just get better and better until you merge.

36
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.030
<v Speaker 1>It is a sheer leap.

37
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:25.390
<v Speaker 1>And that leap shifts the center of consciousness entirely away from the one who invokes.

38
00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:26.230
<v Speaker 2>I don't buy that yet.

39
00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:30.030
<v Speaker 2>How can forgetting yourself in prayer be the height of remembering God?

40
00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:35.790
<v Speaker 2>If the cells must be forgotten, can't a person just concentrate harder to block out their ego?

41
00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.030
<v Speaker 1>That's such a natural reaction to this.

42
00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.470
<v Speaker 1>I mean, we are so conditioned to rely on our willpower.

43
00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.790
<v Speaker 2>Well, we see it in extreme mental discipline all the time.

44
00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:48.470
<v Speaker 2>Athletes, ascetics, they can just force themselves to block out pain or block out distractions.

45
00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.830
<v Speaker 2>So why not just will the ego away?

46
00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:55.710
<v Speaker 1>It sounds plausible, but the text dismantles that idea completely.

47
00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.510
<v Speaker 1>Because there is a paradox built into the conscious will.

48
00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:02.190
<v Speaker 1>If you sit down and tell yourself, I am going to completely forget myself now.

49
00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.910
<v Speaker 2>You are actively thinking about yourself.

50
00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.150
<v Speaker 1>You are entrenching the self at the center of your mind.

51
00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:14.750
<v Speaker 1>Any effort to force yourself into unconsciousness of yourself is by definition an act of conscious will.

52
00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:18.110
<v Speaker 2>It is like an eye trying to wrestle another eye to the ground.

53
00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:21.870
<v Speaker 1>Which just builds a highly disciplined, spiritually muscular ego.

54
00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:23.710
<v Speaker 1>But it is still an ego.

55
00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.990
<v Speaker 1>It is the trap of trying to use the disease as the cure.

56
00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:27.470
<v Speaker 2>I see.

57
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.310
<v Speaker 2>It is reminiscent of insomnia.

58
00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:34.510
<v Speaker 2>If you lie awake and fiercely demand that your brain go to sleep, the demand itself keeps you awake.

59
00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.750
<v Speaker 1>The mechanism of intention traps you.

60
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:41.310
<v Speaker 1>So the text argues that self-forgetting cannot be an assault on the ego.

61
00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.270
<v Speaker 1>You cannot force it.

62
00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:43.230
<v Speaker 2>Then how does it happen?

63
00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.150
<v Speaker 2>If I can't will it, how do I get there?

64
00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.670
<v Speaker 1>It operates as a byproduct.

65
00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.230
<v Speaker 1>It is a byproduct of total absorption in the name.

66
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:54.910
<v Speaker 1>All that polishing we talked about, the human act of invocation that clears the rust, it prepares the ground.

67
00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:59.950
<v Speaker 1>But the ultimate consummation is a divine grace that surpasses all individual effort.

68
00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:03.230
<v Speaker 1>You can walk to the edge of the cliff, but you cannot push yourself off.

69
00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:04.430
<v Speaker 2>The falling is grace.

70
00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:10.670
<v Speaker 1>The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, talked about the people who experienced this.

71
00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:12.150
<v Speaker 1>He called them the outstrippers.

72
00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:15.070
<v Speaker 2>The outstrippers, like someone pulling ahead in a race.

73
00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:20.110
<v Speaker 1>He used imamate of a massive caravan of humanity traveling the road to God.

74
00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.270
<v Speaker 1>The outstrippers are the men and women who remember God abundantly.

75
00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.710
<v Speaker 1>And they have gone on far ahead of the rest of the travelers.

76
00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.470
<v Speaker 1>But this going ahead is not about the physical distance covered.

77
00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.030
<v Speaker 2>It is not about accumulating more spiritual miles than anyone else.

78
00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:36.830
<v Speaker 1>It is about the self left behind.

79
00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.070
<v Speaker 1>They have outstripped their own egos.

80
00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:43.150
<v Speaker 2>The early Sufi master has a reading of scripture that echoes this perfectly.

81
00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.910
<v Speaker 2>He looks at the command, remember your Lord when you have forgotten.

82
00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.230
<v Speaker 2>And his reading is fascinating.

83
00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:54.110
<v Speaker 2>He says that forgetting everything other than God is the true remembrance of God.

84
00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.750
<v Speaker 1>The truest remembrance requires the deepest forgetting.

85
00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:01.710
<v Speaker 1>And the final, most stubborn thing to forget is the you who is doing the remembering.

86
00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:06.750
<v Speaker 2>If we truly let go of our individuality, doesn't that mean we just disappear into the hoid?

87
00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.110
<v Speaker 2>That extinction is just annihilation into nothing.

88
00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:13.070
<v Speaker 1>That is the existential terror at the heart of this.

89
00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.390
<v Speaker 1>When we hear the word extinction, our biological alarms start blaring.

90
00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:22.270
<v Speaker 1>We think losing our individual boundary means falling into an empty abyss.

91
00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.470
<v Speaker 2>Because we equate our boundary with our existence.

92
00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.750
<v Speaker 2>I end here and the world begins there.

93
00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.590
<v Speaker 2>If I draw off the boundary, I feel like I just cease to be.

94
00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:36.990
<v Speaker 1>But the text asks us to look closely at what a boundary actually is.

95
00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:42.430
<v Speaker 1>To be an individual, a specific, limited person is inherently A negation.

96
00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:43.670
<v Speaker 2>A negation of what though?

97
00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:44.390
<v Speaker 1>Of everything else.

98
00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:50.670
<v Speaker 1>To be you sitting in that chair, you have to actively not be the room, not be the sky, to not be the person next to you.

99
00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.750
<v Speaker 1>Your identity is carved out by restricting yourself from the rest of reality.

100
00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.830
<v Speaker 2>So my individuality is actually a restriction.

101
00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:57.870
<v Speaker 1>It is a limitation.

102
00:05:58.030 --> 00:05:59.030
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, to negate

103
00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.590
<v Speaker 1>that limit, which is what extinction is, not a fall into nothingness.

104
00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:04.110
<v Speaker 1>It is the negation of a negation.

105
00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:07.150
<v Speaker 1>And logically, that results in pure affirmation.

106
00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:09.470
<v Speaker 2>The self dissolves, but reality doesn't.

107
00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:12.830
<v Speaker 1>The boundary keeping reality bounded is just removed.

108
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:16.430
<v Speaker 1>The text gives us this governing analogy and it is crucial.

109
00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:22.350
<v Speaker 1>Think of God's reality as the light and creation as the shadow that follows after.

110
00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:23.150
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I'm with you.

111
00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:24.670
<v Speaker 2>Light and shadow.

112
00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.550
<v Speaker 1>If you are the shadow, you feel very real to yourself.

113
00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.310
<v Speaker 1>You have a shape, you move.

114
00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:31.950
<v Speaker 1>But a shadow is just a deprivation of light.

115
00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.710
<v Speaker 1>It has no substance of its own.

116
00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:37.150
<v Speaker 1>Now, if you are that shadow, you are terrified of dissolving.

117
00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.390
<v Speaker 2>Because you think fading away means dying.

118
00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.310
<v Speaker 1>But why does a shadow dissolve?

119
00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:45.550
<v Speaker 1>It dissolves because the light has moved directly overhead and banished the darkness.

120
00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:48.270
<v Speaker 1>Dissolving the shadow does not diminish reality.

121
00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.230
<v Speaker 1>It reveals the blinding light that was always there.

122
00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:52.910
<v Speaker 2>The substance remains.

123
00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:54.910
<v Speaker 2>The secondary consequence fades.

124
00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:58.110
<v Speaker 1>You do not vanish into a void, you vanish into the absolute.

125
00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:01.630
<v Speaker 2>The Andalusian master talks about this using the story of Moses on the mountain.

126
00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:02.430
<v Speaker 1>He does.

127
00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:09.230
<v Speaker 1>In his treatise on the subject, he examines that terrifying moment when Moses asked to see God and then fell down stunned or dead.

128
00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:12.030
<v Speaker 2>The mountain shatters and Moses collapses.

129
00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:15.750
<v Speaker 1>The Andalusian master describes A visionary encounter where he asks

130
00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:16.910
<v Speaker 1>Moses about this.

131
00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.630
<v Speaker 1>And Moses confirms A devastating truth.

132
00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:22.510
<v Speaker 1>He could only see God while he was dead.

133
00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:24.910
<v Speaker 2>While the individual structure of Moses was gone.

134
00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:28.350
<v Speaker 1>The vision of God is only possible where the self is not.

135
00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:33.470
<v Speaker 1>The finite structure of a human individual cannot contain the infinite reality.

136
00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:36.350
<v Speaker 1>It shatters it, like pouring the ocean into a teacup.

137
00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:38.830
<v Speaker 2>So there is a death before physical death.

138
00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:42.910
<v Speaker 1>The reviver jurist maps out the extreme edge of this process.

139
00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.630
<v Speaker 1>He calls it the extinction of extinction.

140
00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:47.710
<v Speaker 2>Okay, we know what extinction is.

141
00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:48.990
<v Speaker 2>The fading of the self.

142
00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:50.990
<v Speaker 2>What is extinguishing the extinction?

143
00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.550
<v Speaker 1>It is the final trap of the ego.

144
00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:56.670
<v Speaker 1>In the first stage of extinction, the self goes quiet.

145
00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:57.870
<v Speaker 1>The noise stops.

146
00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:05.110
<v Speaker 1>But if the soul sits there and realizes it is quiet, if it is conscious of its own unconsciousness, it is still observing itself.

147
00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.790
<v Speaker 2>There's an observer saying, look how deeply I am concentrating.

148
00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:09.190
<v Speaker 2>Look how quiet I am.

149
00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:12.510
<v Speaker 1>Which means the ego is still there, just hiding in the observer's chair.

150
00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.750
<v Speaker 1>So the extinction of extinction happens when the self becomes unconscious.

151
00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:17.390
<v Speaker 1>of its own unconsciousness.

152
00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.710
<v Speaker 1>The observer is severed from the observed.

153
00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:23.950
<v Speaker 2>The text mentions the loneliness of unconsciousness as a prerequisite here.

154
00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:27.710
<v Speaker 1>You have to surrender your comfortable, self-satisfied intimacy.

155
00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:36.190
<v Speaker 1>You have to give up the warm feeling of being a pious person who is doing a good job at praying to taste the true intimacy of remembrance.

156
00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:39.230
<v Speaker 2>That brings up a massive structural problem, though.

157
00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:43.550
<v Speaker 2>If the human agent is entirely gone, if the self is erased,

158
00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:46.270
<v Speaker 2>The observer is gone, who is doing the remembering?

159
00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:52.030
<v Speaker 1>This brings us to the grand inversion of the entire process, and it is the core of the Imam's teaching.

160
00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.910
<v Speaker 1>The invocation comes forth firstly from the invoked and secondly from the invoker.

161
00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:58.350
<v Speaker 2>It flips everything upside down.

162
00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.590
<v Speaker 1>Let's unpack the order of being here.

163
00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:08.190
<v Speaker 1>Usually we picture prayer as a creature standing on the earth, calling out across this massive, silent void, hoping to reach a distant God.

164
00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:10.590
<v Speaker 1>We think we're starting the conversation.

165
00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.590
<v Speaker 2>We think our voice is the origin point.

166
00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:19.990
<v Speaker 1>The reality is that God's remembrance of the human being precedes the human's remembrance of God, and not just chronologically, but in the.

167
00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.030
<v Speaker 1>fundamental order of existence.

168
00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:25.070
<v Speaker 2>How does God's remembrance function as the origin?

169
00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:26.510
<v Speaker 1>Think about creation.

170
00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:30.430
<v Speaker 1>God's creative word calls the human out of nothingness.

171
00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:34.590
<v Speaker 1>Before you existed, you were a possibility known only by God.

172
00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:40.430
<v Speaker 1>The act of holding you in existence moment by moment, giving you the breath to even form a thought.

173
00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:42.350
<v Speaker 2>That is the primary invocation.

174
00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:46.110
<v Speaker 1>God remembering the creature is what allows the creature to exist.

175
00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:50.270
<v Speaker 1>If God ceased to remember you, would simply blink out of being.

176
00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:51.710
<v Speaker 2>I see the inversion now.

177
00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:54.190
<v Speaker 2>The capacity to call out was the first gift.

178
00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:59.310
<v Speaker 2>Our human response is just the shadow of God's prior call holding us in existence.

179
00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.750
<v Speaker 1>The breath you use to say the name is borrowed from the source.

180
00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:08.830
<v Speaker 1>To understand the mechanics of this, the text introduces another vital image, the clear mirror.

181
00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.630
<v Speaker 2>So we are back to the polished heart.

182
00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:15.070
<v Speaker 1>The human invocation is merely the reflection thrown back into the mirror of the heart.

183
00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:17.230
<v Speaker 1>Now, think about a flawless mirror.

184
00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:20.870
<v Speaker 1>When you look into a perfectly clean mirror, you don't see the mirror itself.

185
00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:21.870
<v Speaker 1>You don't see the glass.

186
00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:24.670
<v Speaker 2>You see the glass, it means there are smudges or scratches on it.

187
00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:29.550
<v Speaker 1>But if the rest of the ego is entirely gone, nothing of the glass intrudes.

188
00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:31.070
<v Speaker 1>Only the face is seen.

189
00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.790
<v Speaker 1>To remember God is to step out of the way.

190
00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:38.430
<v Speaker 1>It is to let God remember himself, holding creation and being

191
00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:42.830
<v Speaker 1>to the very creature who previously thought they were the one doing the work.

192
00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.990
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay isn't calling a St.

193
00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:47.870
<v Speaker 2>one of God's names, just blasphemy dressed up?

194
00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:48.270
<v Speaker 1>Yeah.

195
00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:57.390
<v Speaker 2>Especially since the 6th imam who denies the name is the named, argued explicitly against this to prevent people from worshiping 99 different gods.

196
00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:00.750
<v Speaker 1>That is the ultimate friction point, and it is a brilliant question.

197
00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:05.470
<v Speaker 1>We are talking about total absorption in the name, but we have to ask, what actually is the name?

198
00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:08.030
<v Speaker 1>Is it just an arbitrary human label?

199
00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:10.590
<v Speaker 1>Is it just a sound we invented to point at the sky?

200
00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:12.430
<v Speaker 1>Or is it reality itself?

201
00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:20.110
<v Speaker 2>If the name is the named, as the Sufi maxim says, that feels like a collision with the warning from the 60 mom who denies the name is the named.

202
00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:25.710
<v Speaker 1>The Andalusian master resolves this by showing us that the word name operates in two entirely different senses.

203
00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:30.110
<v Speaker 1>If we conflate them, we end up in heresy or a totally hollow theology.

204
00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:31.630
<v Speaker 2>Okay, let's look at the first sense.

205
00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:34.190
<v Speaker 2>The name as a conceptual boundary.

